{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186311568 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=xz+1,\\, && y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=y+w-x,\\, && z\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=-yz,\\, && w\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(0),$ $z(0),$ and $w(0).$\n\n$\\,y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(0)≈$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186311569 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2y-z+x,\\, && y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} &=z+w,\\, && z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\frac{xz}{2y},\\, && w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186311570 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=xy+z,\\, && y\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=x+w,\\, && z\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=3 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=-yz,\\, && w\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186311571 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=y+z+w,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=xy-{w}^{2},\\, && z(0)=-2 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\frac{yz}{w},\\, && w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ $z\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and $w\\,(\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n$\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186311572 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=1-2y+z,\\, && y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=2+x-w,\\, && z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=yz,\\, && w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186311573 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=1+x-2y+3z,\\, && y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=2y-w,\\, && z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=-x+yz,\\, && w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186311574 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2-x+z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=3y-2z+w,\\, && z(0)=-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=1-y,\\, && w(0)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186311575 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=(x+1)(y-1)+z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=2-(x-1)(z-2),\\, && z(0)=2 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\sqrt{x+{w}^{2}}-\\frac{y}{2},\\, && w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186311576 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &={x}^{2}+3y-z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{x+1}{w}-y,\\, && z(0)=0 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\sqrt[3]{y+z+7},\\, && w(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186311577 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=\\sqrt[3]{x+y-1}+z,\\, && y(0)=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{{y}^{2}-1}{w+1},\\, && z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′} &=\\sqrt{x+{z}^{2}}-y,\\, && w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186311578 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186311578 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1+v(t)+1-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+3(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+3v(t)+3-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186311580 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-x(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186311580 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-x(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2u(t)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+3(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+3v(t)+6-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186311581 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186311581 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+3(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+1)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+3u(t)-3+v(t)+1+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-3(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+2(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+2v(t)+2-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186311582 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t)-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186311582 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t)-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+2)^{2}-3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)-3u(t)-3-v(t)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-8 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+5(v(t)+2)-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+5v(t)+10-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186311583 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186311583 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+4(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+2)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+4u(t)-4+v(t)+2+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+8(v(t)+2)-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+8v(t)+16-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186311590 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=y(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(4) \\\\ y(4)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186311591 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=7x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=7y(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-3) \\\\ y(-3)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186311592 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-8x(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-1) \\\\ y(-1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186311593 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186311594 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [17592186311602 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [17592186311602 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1 &=0 \\\\ 2x-2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186311604 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[3,\\,0]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186311604 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+3,\\,y(t)=v(t),\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+3)^{2}-6(u(t)+3)+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+6u(t)+9)-6u(t)-18+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+3)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+12-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t) &={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) &=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"]}
